FM_CTRIES.push.apply(FM_CTRIES, [["us", "United States", "🇺🇸"], ["uk", "United Kingdom", "🇬🇧"], ["ca", "Canada", "🇨🇦"], ["au", "Australia", "🇦🇺"], ["de", "Germany", "🇩🇪"], ["fr", "France", "🇫🇷"], ["il", "Israel", "🇮🇱"]]);

/* K9 TCCC: care for military working dogs (owner, Oct 29 2026). Education only; veterinary direction governs care. */
var FM_K9_TOPICS = [
  ["Why medics treat dogs", "🐕", "Military working dogs (MWDs) detect explosives and drugs, patrol and track alongside the units they serve. When a dog is hurt far forward, the closest trained provider is often a medic, corpsman or PJ rather than a veterinarian.\nThe Joint Trauma System (JTS) K9 Healthcare Provider Responsibilities CPG describes human healthcare providers giving emergency care to an MWD when the dog is too unstable to move, evacuation is not possible and veterinary personnel are not available. That care is limited to the provider's training and to threats to life, limb or eyesight, plus preparing the dog for evacuation to veterinary care.\nRoutine medical, dental and surgical care stays with the Veterinary Corps. Veterinary staff are notified as soon as possible, and their direction, by phone, radio or telemedicine, governs care. The Canine Tactical Combat Casualty Care (K9TCCC) Guidelines from the Committee on TCCC set out the prehospital approach; text in italics in those guidelines marks where dog care differs from human TCCC."],
  ["Normal vital signs", "❤️", "Dogs differ from people in nearly every number. Normal rectal temperature runs about 101 to 102.5 F (38.3 to 39.2 C (100.9-102.6 F)), higher than a human's, and the JTS heat injury CPG notes working dogs commonly reach around 106 F (41.1 C (106 F)) during hard work and recover normally.\nResting heart rate in a large working dog is roughly 60 to 100 beats per minute (the JTS MWD CPG lists 60 to 80 at rest); excitement, pain and heat push it well above that. Many dogs have a normal sinus arrhythmia, a rate that rises and falls with breathing.\nResting respiratory rate is about 10 to 30 breaths per minute. Panting is a normal cooling mechanism and makes a breath count unreliable; effort, noise and posture (neck extended, elbows out) tell more.\nCapillary refill time is under 2 seconds on the gums. Mucous membranes are normally pink and moist; pale, gray, brick-red, blue or yellow membranes are abnormal. Many dogs have pigmented (black) gums, so the inner lip, conjunctiva, prepuce or vulva serves instead.\nThe femoral pulse is felt on the inside of the thigh in the groove where the femoral artery runs. A weak or absent femoral pulse, or a pulse that does not match the heartbeat (pulse deficit), signals poor perfusion. Pulse oximeter probes fit the tongue, an unpigmented lip, ear flap, prepuce or vulva. Blood pressure in a normal dog is near that of a person, with a systolic pressure around 120 mmHg."],
  ["Handler first, muzzle second", "🦮", "The handler is the dog's first responder and the person the dog trusts. Handlers are trained in K9 first aid, know the dog's baseline behavior and normal values, and usually carry a K9 aid kit. The handler typically controls the dog's head while the medic works.\nA hurt, frightened dog bites, even one that is gentle at baseline, and many MWDs are trained to bite. The K9TCCC Guidelines describe muzzling to protect providers unless the tactical situation prevents it or the dog has airway obstruction or breathing difficulty.\nA muzzle is contraindicated or removed when the dog is struggling to breathe, vomiting or likely to vomit, overheated (panting is how a dog sheds heat), has facial or jaw injury, or is unconscious or intubated. Opioids commonly cause vomiting in dogs, so a muzzled dog is watched closely after opioid dosing. A basket muzzle lets a dog pant and drink; a tight cloth or tape muzzle does not. If there is no airway concern, the muzzle typically stays on during transport."],
  ["Restraint and safety", "🛡️", "Safe restraint protects both the team and the dog. The handler is the preferred restrainer, and the provider's face stays away from the dog's head. Two people working together, one controlling the head and one working on the body, is the common approach.\nA dog lying on its side (lateral recumbency) with the handler holding the head and front legs gives access to the chest, limbs and veins. A conscious dog that resists is not forced into one position; chest-injured and bloated dogs often choose the position in which they breathe best.\nAn MWD may still respond to commands, so the handler, not a stranger, gives commands. If the handler is also a casualty, another trained handler or the kennel master is ideal; when no one familiar is available, a calm, quiet approach, a leash or catch-pole and a muzzle reduce risk. Sedation of a dangerous dog is a veterinary-directed decision."],
  ["K9 MARCH-PAWS differences", "🧭", "The K9TCCC Guidelines follow the same phases as human TCCC (care under fire, tactical field care, tactical evacuation care) and a similar MARCH sequence, with key differences.\nM, massive hemorrhage: windlass limb tourniquets designed for humans tend to slip on the cone-shaped dog limb, so an elastic, stretch-wrap tourniquet is the only one the guidelines consider; junctional tourniquets and pelvic binders are not recommended. Packing with hemostatic gauze and pressure is central.\nA, airway: cervical spine stabilization is not required, and nasopharyngeal airways, blind-insertion airways and extraglottic devices are not used. The surgical airway is made between tracheal rings, not through the cricothyroid membrane.\nR, respiration: needle decompression is at the side of the chest, not the front, and both sides are commonly considered.\nC, circulation: only canine blood products are used; human blood products cause transfusion reactions.\nH, hypothermia and head injury: dogs also face heat injury, and rectal temperature is tracked.\nIn PAWS, pain is scored on a 0 to 10 scale using canine behavior descriptors, antibiotics are part of care for open combat wounds, wounds and burns are dressed, and splints are applied when helpful."],
  ["Airway and breathing", "🫁", "The dog's airway is long, and the larynx is easy to see when the tongue is pulled forward, so orotracheal intubation under direct vision is the main advanced airway. Endotracheal tubes in large working dogs are much larger than in people, up to about 10 mm internal diameter in the K9TCCC Guidelines. An intubated dog is kept deeply anesthetized, because a waking dog chews and bites the tube.\nThe K9 surgical airway differs from the human cricothyroidotomy: the incision is made on the front midline of the neck between tracheal rings (rings 3 and 4 or 4 and 5 in the guidelines), and the cut is limited to about half the trachea's circumference. Tracheostomy tubes up to about 8 mm are described.\nBag-valve ventilation in the guidelines runs at about 8 to 10 breaths per minute. Conscious dogs receive blow-by oxygen; tight masks trap heat and humidity and are poorly tolerated.\nDog mediastinum is thin and often communicates between sides, so a pneumothorax is frequently bilateral. Needle decompression in the K9TCCC Guidelines is on the side of the chest at the 7th to 9th intercostal space, about midway up the chest wall, with the needle angled toward the head and the catheter fed off the needle rather than driven to the hub."],
  ["Hemorrhage control", "🩸", "Dog limbs are wide at the top and narrow toward the paw, and loose skin slides over muscle, so a rigid windlass tourniquet tends to slip down the leg and lose pressure. The K9TCCC Guidelines consider only a stretchable elastic tourniquet (for example, the SWAT-T) for massive limb bleeding; it works as a tight circumferential pressure wrap.\nThe tail is a common and bloody wound site, especially in dogs that wag against walls or kennel doors (happy tail), and tail wounds are typically controlled with direct pressure and a snug wrap; veterinary references describe an elastic wrap near the tail base for severe bleeding.\nJunctional areas, the armpit (axilla), groin and neck, are packed with hemostatic gauze and held with firm pressure. Junctional tourniquets and pelvic binders are not recommended for dogs. Wound clamps that close skin edges are described as an option.\nTimes of tourniquet application, conversion and removal are recorded on the K9TCCC Card."],
  ["IV and IO access", "💉", "The cephalic vein, running along the front of the forearm, is the most common IV site and is raised by the handler holding off the vein at the elbow. The lateral saphenous vein on the outside of the hind leg just above the hock, and the medial saphenous on the inside of the thigh, are alternatives. The jugular vein is large but is generally avoided in head injury (compression raises intracranial pressure) and in bleeding disorders. The K9TCCC Guidelines describe an 18-gauge catheter or saline lock as preferred.\nWhen IV access fails, intraosseous access is described at the proximal medial tibia just below the knee (the guideline's preferred site, with a pediatric-type IO device) and at the proximal humerus. Hair is clipped and the skin cleaned before access, and catheters are taped securely because dogs chew lines."],
  ["Fluids and blood", "🧪", "Dogs have their own blood groups, called dog erythrocyte antigens (DEA). DEA 1 (historically split into 1.1 and 1.2) is the most important clinically; DEA 1-negative dogs are favored as donors. Unlike people, dogs generally lack strong naturally occurring antibodies, so a first mismatched transfusion is often tolerated, but the dog becomes sensitized and a later mismatched transfusion can cause a severe reaction. That is why the K9TCCC Guidelines call for veterinary direction for a dog transfused more than 72 hours before injury.\nHuman blood products are not given to dogs because of a high risk of transfusion reaction; human albumin is also avoided. The resuscitation preference in the guidelines is canine whole blood, then canine plasma and red cells in a 1:1 ratio, then plasma or red cells alone, then a balanced crystalloid (lactated Ringer's, Normosol-R or Plasma-Lyte A). Calcium is given after the second transfused product. Canine blood products are kept by veterinary facilities and are rarely in a medic's kit.\nHetastarch (Hextend) appears in the older JTS MWD CPG as a colloid option, but it was removed from the K9TCCC burn resuscitation recommendations and does not appear in the current shock fluid sequence; veterinary concern about kidney injury and clotting effects has grown."],
  ["Heat and cold", "🌡️", "Dogs sweat very little and shed heat mainly by panting, which fails in hot, humid air, under body armor or with a tight muzzle. Heat injury is among the leading medical causes of MWD death. The JTS K9 heat injury CPG notes fatal heat stroke at rectal temperatures as low as about 105.8 F, while healthy working dogs can reach 106 F, so signs matter as much as the number.\nRectal temperature is the field standard; it can lag core temperature by up to about 15 minutes. Cooling in the CPG means soaking the coat through to the skin with tepid water, fans and a cooler space. Ice-water immersion is not part of that guidance. Active cooling stops at about 103 to 103.5 F because rebound hypothermia is common.\nHypothermia also matters after blood loss, immersion or anesthesia. The K9TCCC Guidelines describe removing wet gear, patting the coat dry, insulating from the ground, an active heating blanket on the torso without direct contact with skin, an enclosed hypothermia wrap and warmed IV or IO fluids."],
  ["Evacuation and records", "🚁", "The K9TCCC Card (DD Form 3073) travels with the dog through every handoff, like the human DD 1380. It records vital signs, interventions, tourniquet times and medications; vital signs are recorded at least hourly. The DD Form 3074 Canine Treatment and Resuscitation Record is used for longer resuscitation. At the end of care the card goes into the dog's veterinary record and to the DoD Military Working Dog Trauma Registry.\nThe handler and dog travel together whenever feasible. The handler calms the dog, helps restrain it and knows its history, and a separated dog is harder and riskier to manage. Destination is a veterinary treatment facility; the Daniel E. Holland Military Working Dog Hospital at Joint Base San Antonio-Lackland is the referral center for the most serious cases.\nThe handler is often emotionally bonded to the dog as a partner and may be injured too. Handlers can be distressed, angry or reluctant to let others touch the dog. Keeping the handler informed and involved, and checking on the handler afterward, is part of caring for the team."],
  ["What differs most about drugs", "💊", "Drug response in dogs is not a scaled-down human response. Dogs often need different per-weight doses (opioid doses for dogs are considerably higher than human doses), and some common human medicines are dangerous to them.\nHuman over-the-counter NSAIDs such as ibuprofen and naproxen cause stomach ulcers and kidney injury in dogs at relatively low exposures. Xylitol, a sweetener in sugar-free gum and some medications, drops blood sugar in dogs. Combination cold products with pseudoephedrine or phenylephrine are toxic to dogs.\nOpioids commonly cause vomiting and panting in dogs, ketamine can raise eye pressure, and midazolam alone can excite rather than calm. Naloxone is kept on hand whenever opioids are used.\nThe K9TCCC Guidelines list specific drugs for pain, sedation, antibiotics and resuscitation, and the JTS MWD CPG adds drugs for seizures, cardiac arrest and brain swelling. Drug selection and dosing for a dog are set by veterinary guidance and the current CPG, not by human dosing."]
];

var FM_K9_COND = [
  { id: "k9-gdv", name: "Gastric dilatation-volvulus (bloat)", line: "A twisted, gas-filled stomach that kills within hours",
    what: "The stomach fills with gas and fluid (dilatation) and may rotate on its long axis (volvulus), closing both ends and twisting the spleen and its blood supply. Large, deep-chested breeds such as German Shepherds, Belgian Malinois and Labrador Retrievers are at highest risk.",
    why: "The swollen stomach presses on the caudal vena cava and portal vein, cutting venous return and causing obstructive and distributive shock. The stomach wall can die, and toxins and arrhythmias follow. Many MWDs receive a preventive gastropexy (the stomach is tacked to the body wall), which greatly reduces twisting but does not prevent gas distension. MWDs are also at risk of mesenteric volvulus, a twisting of the intestine that is even more rapidly fatal.",
    signs: ["Unproductive retching or attempts to vomit with little or nothing coming up", "Swollen, tight, drum-like belly behind the ribs, often more on the left", "Drooling, restlessness, pacing, looking at the belly, standing with the head low", "Rapid heart rate, pale gums, slow capillary refill, weak femoral pulses", "Collapse in late stages"],
    care: ["Treated as a surgical emergency, with immediate notification of veterinary staff and rapid evacuation", "Large-bore IV access, often in both cephalic veins (forelimbs), with fluid resuscitation for shock", "Gastric decompression by orogastric tube or needle (trocar) decompression through the flank is a veterinary-directed procedure", "Oxygen, with a muzzle avoided or removed because of retching", "Heart rhythm and perfusion monitored; arrhythmias are common, even hours after correction"],
    warn: ["Food or water by mouth is withheld", "Hind-limb IV sites are less useful because abdominal pressure limits venous return from the rear", "A gastropexy history does not rule out bloat"] },

  { id: "k9-heat", name: "Heat injury and heat stroke", line: "Panting fails, temperature climbs, organs fail",
    what: "A spectrum from heat stress to heat stroke, in which body temperature rises beyond the dog's ability to shed heat and causes organ injury. Exertion in heat and humidity, body armor, crates or vehicles without airflow, obesity and poor acclimation all add risk.",
    why: "Dogs depend on panting and airflow to cool. Above a threshold, heat damages the gut lining, kidneys, liver, brain and blood vessels, and the clotting system can fail (disseminated intravascular coagulation). Damage continues after cooling, so a dog that looks better can still deteriorate over the next 24 to 72 hours.",
    signs: ["Rectal temperature well above normal, often above 105 to 106 F, though no single number defines heat stroke", "Heavy panting, thick ropy saliva, brick-red then pale or gray gums", "Weakness, stumbling, collapse, confusion, seizures", "Vomiting and diarrhea, sometimes bloody", "Bruising or pinpoint bleeding on the gums or belly (clotting failure)"],
    care: ["Work stops and the dog is moved to shade with airflow", "The JTS heat CPG describes soaking the coat to the skin with tepid water plus fans; ice water is not part of that guidance", "Active cooling stops at a rectal temperature of about 103 to 103.5 F, followed by drying and close temperature monitoring", "Room-temperature IV crystalloid, oxygen by blow-by and blood glucose checks are described in the CPG", "Urgent evacuation to veterinary care for any dog with suspected heat stroke, even after cooling"],
    warn: ["A tight muzzle or oxygen mask blocks panting and heat loss", "Wet towels draped and left on the dog trap heat", "Low blood sugar, kidney injury and bleeding can appear later"] },

  { id: "k9-blast", name: "Gunshot and blast wounds", line: "Same physics, different anatomy and armor gaps",
    what: "Penetrating, blast and fragment injuries in MWDs, which often work ahead of the patrol. Canine body armor covers the chest and part of the abdomen, leaving the head, neck and limbs exposed.",
    why: "Limbs, chest and abdomen are frequently injured. A dog's thick coat hides wounds, and loose skin can let an entry wound sit far from deeper damage. Blast can cause lung injury, ruptured eardrums, abdominal injury and traumatic brain injury.",
    signs: ["Bleeding or matted, wet hair over a wound", "Lameness or a limb not bearing weight", "Fast or labored breathing, abdominal swelling or pain", "Pale gums, slow capillary refill, rapid weak pulse", "Head shaking, deafness or ear bleeding after blast"],
    care: ["MARCH sequence adapted per the K9TCCC Guidelines, after muzzling when breathing allows", "Hands and gloves run through the coat to find all wounds; hair may be clipped around wounds", "Elastic tourniquet for limb hemorrhage, hemostatic packing for junctional wounds", "Antibiotics for open combat wounds as listed in the K9TCCC Guidelines", "Pain scored on the canine 0 to 10 behavior scale and treated under the guidelines"],
    warn: ["Windlass human tourniquets often slip on dog limbs", "Missed wounds under the coat are common", "Dogs mask pain; a quiet dog may be in severe pain"] },

  { id: "k9-shock", name: "Hemorrhagic shock", line: "Gums and femoral pulse tell the story",
    what: "Inadequate tissue perfusion from blood loss. Dogs compensate strongly with heart rate and vasoconstriction, then decompensate quickly.",
    why: "A dog's spleen is a large blood reservoir that contracts in early shock and briefly raises red cell mass, which can make early blood counts look reassuring. Ongoing loss leads to falling blood pressure, acidosis, hypothermia and coagulopathy, as in people.",
    signs: ["Heart rate above the dog's normal range, often above 140 to 160 in a large dog", "Pale or white gums, capillary refill over 2 seconds", "Weak or absent femoral pulses, cold limbs", "Rectal temperature dropping", "Dull mental state, then collapse"],
    care: ["Hemorrhage controlled first", "IV or IO access with resuscitation following the K9TCCC sequence: canine whole blood, then canine plasma and red cells 1:1, then plasma or red cells alone, then balanced crystalloid", "Calcium after the second transfused product, per the guidelines", "TXA considered under the guidelines when significant transfusion is expected, within 3 hours of injury", "Active prevention of hypothermia"],
    warn: ["Human blood products are not given to dogs", "Large crystalloid volumes dilute clotting factors and worsen bleeding", "Veterinary direction needed for a dog transfused more than 72 hours earlier"] },

  { id: "k9-chest", name: "Tension pneumothorax and penetrating chest wounds", line: "One hole can collapse both lungs",
    what: "Air entering the chest through a wound or a torn lung and building pressure. In dogs the mediastinum is thin and often incomplete, so a pneumothorax frequently involves both sides.",
    why: "Rising pressure collapses the lungs and blocks venous return to the heart. Dogs with chest injury often sit or stand with elbows out and neck extended and resist lying on their side.",
    signs: ["Fast, shallow or labored breathing; open-mouth breathing with effort, not relaxed panting", "Elbows held away from the body, neck extended, reluctance to lie down", "Reduced breath sounds, often on both sides", "Blue or gray gums, falling SpO2", "Sucking or bubbling chest wound"],
    care: ["Muzzle avoided or removed because of breathing difficulty", "Open chest wounds sealed with a vented chest seal", "Needle decompression in the K9TCCC Guidelines at the 7th to 9th intercostal space on the side of the chest, midway up, angled toward the head, catheter advanced off the needle", "Both sides commonly decompressed because of shared pleural space", "Oxygen by blow-by and the dog allowed to keep the position it chooses"],
    warn: ["The human front-of-chest site does not apply to dogs", "Driving a long needle to the hub risks lung and vessel injury", "Older JTS guidance listed the 6th to 8th intercostal space; current K9TCCC text is the reference"] },

  { id: "k9-tbi", name: "Traumatic brain injury", line: "Protect pressure, oxygen and the jugular veins",
    what: "Brain injury from blast, blunt impact or penetrating wounds. Dogs are assessed with the Modified Glasgow Coma Scale (MGCS), which scores motor activity, brainstem reflexes and level of consciousness from 3 to 18, with higher scores better.",
    why: "As in humans, low blood pressure and low oxygen cause secondary brain injury. Compression of the jugular veins in the neck, from collars, wraps or blood draws, raises intracranial pressure.",
    signs: ["Dullness, stupor or coma", "Unequal or fixed pupils, abnormal eye movements", "Abnormal posture, seizures, circling", "Bleeding from the nose or ears", "Slow heart rate with high blood pressure in late stages"],
    care: ["Oxygen, with SpO2 above 90 percent, systolic pressure above 90 mmHg and end-tidal CO2 of 35 to 40 mmHg as targets in the K9TCCC Guidelines", "Head elevated slightly on a flat board without bending or compressing the neck", "Hypertonic saline described in the guidelines for signs of impending herniation", "TXA described in the guidelines for significant head injury within 3 hours", "Seizures managed under veterinary guidance"],
    warn: ["Collars, neck bandages and jugular catheters or blood draws are avoided", "Steroids are not a TBI treatment", "Repeat MGCS scores track trends better than one score"] },

  { id: "k9-choc", name: "Chocolate, caffeine, grapes and raisins", line: "Ordinary snacks that poison dogs",
    what: "Chocolate contains theobromine and caffeine (methylxanthines). Dark and baking chocolate and cocoa powder contain far more than milk chocolate, and caffeine pills, energy products and coffee grounds are also concentrated sources. Grapes, raisins and currants can cause acute kidney injury in dogs; the ASPCA Animal Poison Control Center has identified tartaric acid as the likely toxic substance.",
    why: "Dogs clear theobromine slowly, so effects last a day or longer and can include dangerous heart rhythms and seizures. Grape and raisin sensitivity varies widely and unpredictably between dogs, so no amount is considered safe.",
    signs: ["Methylxanthines: vomiting, restlessness, hyperactivity, panting, rapid heart rate, tremors, high temperature, seizures", "Signs may start hours after chocolate is eaten", "Grapes or raisins: vomiting within hours, then lethargy, loss of appetite and reduced urine output over 1 to 3 days"],
    care: ["Poison control or veterinary consultation early, with the product, amount and time of ingestion recorded", "Induced vomiting and activated charcoal are veterinary-directed decisions", "IV fluids and heart rhythm monitoring under veterinary direction", "Kidney monitoring for 48 to 72 hours after grape or raisin exposure"],
    warn: ["A dog with no signs yet can still be poisoned", "Vomiting is not induced in a dog that is seizing, very drowsy or already vomiting", "Trail mix, baked goods and energy bars hide raisins and chocolate"] },

  { id: "k9-xylitol", name: "Xylitol ingestion", line: "Sugar-free gum can drop blood sugar in minutes",
    what: "Xylitol (also sold as birch sugar) is a sweetener in sugar-free gum, mints, some peanut butters, toothpaste, chewable vitamins, nicotine gum and some liquid medications.",
    why: "In dogs, unlike people, xylitol triggers a large and rapid insulin release, causing hypoglycemia within about 10 to 60 minutes. Larger exposures can cause acute liver failure a day or more later.",
    signs: ["Vomiting", "Weakness, stumbling, trembling", "Collapse and seizures from low blood sugar", "Later: yellow gums, bruising or bleeding from liver failure"],
    care: ["Blood glucose checked early and repeatedly", "Dextrose by vein under veterinary direction for low blood sugar", "Veterinary or poison control consultation for decontamination decisions and liver monitoring", "Evacuation for monitoring even if the dog looks well"],
    warn: ["Activated charcoal binds xylitol poorly", "Human-formulated liquid medicines can contain xylitol", "The handler's gum and snacks in the vehicle are common sources"] },

  { id: "k9-painmeds", name: "Human pain reliever ingestion (NSAIDs, acetaminophen)", line: "A few pills from a pill pack can injure a dog",
    what: "Ingestion of human ibuprofen, naproxen, aspirin or acetaminophen, often from an aid bag, pill pack or personal kit. Naproxen stays in a dog's body much longer than in a person.",
    why: "NSAIDs cause stomach and intestinal ulcers and, at higher exposures, acute kidney injury and seizures. Acetaminophen in dogs causes liver injury and methemoglobinemia (blood that cannot carry oxygen) at high exposures and has been linked to dry eye; cats are far more sensitive than dogs.",
    signs: ["NSAIDs: vomiting (sometimes with blood), black tarry stool, abdominal pain, increased thirst and urination", "Acetaminophen: brown or muddy gums, rapid breathing, facial or paw swelling, dark urine, weakness", "Later: yellow gums from liver injury"],
    care: ["Exact product, strength, number of pills and time recorded for poison control or veterinary staff", "Decontamination decisions made under veterinary direction", "Veterinary antidote and protective treatment, such as N-acetylcysteine for acetaminophen and stomach protectants and IV fluids for NSAIDs", "Kidney and liver monitoring over several days"],
    warn: ["Human dosing does not translate to dogs", "Combination cold products may also contain decongestants toxic to dogs", "Veterinary NSAIDs such as meloxicam are not combined with steroids or other NSAIDs"] },

  { id: "k9-opioid", name: "Opioid and fentanyl exposure", line: "Detection dogs work around the drugs they find",
    what: "Exposure of a narcotics detection dog to fentanyl, its analogs or other opioids during a search, usually by inhaling or swallowing powder.",
    why: "Opioids depress the brain and breathing. Dogs often show sedation, slow heart rate, vomiting and low temperature, and severe exposure causes breathing depression. Street supplies may also contain xylazine, a sedative that naloxone does not reverse.",
    signs: ["Sudden drowsiness, stumbling, unresponsiveness", "Slow heart rate, slow or shallow breathing", "Vomiting, drooling", "Low rectal temperature", "Small pupils"],
    care: ["Dog removed from the source, with handlers and medics protected with gloves and respirators per unit policy", "Naloxone by intranasal or intramuscular route is described in veterinary and K9 guidance; handlers often carry K9 naloxone kits", "Ventilation support and oxygen if breathing is inadequate", "Monitoring for re-sedation, since naloxone can wear off before the opioid", "Decontamination of the coat and paws to prevent re-exposure from grooming"],
    warn: ["Exposed handlers may also need naloxone", "Sedation that does not respond may involve xylazine or other agents", "A muzzled sedated dog that vomits can aspirate"] },

  { id: "k9-venom", name: "Snake bite, stings and anaphylaxis", line: "Swelling of the face can close the airway",
    what: "Venomous bites (pit vipers in the U.S.; vipers and elapids such as cobras overseas) and insect stings that can trigger anaphylaxis. Dogs are often bitten on the face, muzzle or front legs while investigating.",
    why: "Viper venom causes swelling, tissue damage, clotting failure and shock; elapid venom can cause paralysis with little local swelling. In dogs, anaphylaxis centers on the liver and gut, so vomiting, diarrhea and sudden collapse are more typical than throat swelling, though facial hives and swelling occur.",
    signs: ["Puncture wounds with rapid, painful swelling and bruising", "Weakness, drooling, trouble breathing or swallowing", "Bleeding from wounds or gums", "Facial swelling, hives, vomiting, diarrhea, pale gums and collapse in anaphylaxis"],
    care: ["Dog kept calm and still; a muzzle is avoided with facial swelling or breathing difficulty", "Collars and harnesses loosened as the neck swells", "Epinephrine for anaphylaxis and antivenom for envenomation are veterinary-directed treatments", "IV access and fluids for shock", "Rapid evacuation to a facility with antivenom when available"],
    warn: ["Tourniquets, cutting, suction and ice are not used for snake bite", "Antihistamines and steroids do not reverse venom effects", "Facial swelling can progress for hours"] },

  { id: "k9-smoke", name: "Smoke inhalation", line: "Carbon monoxide fools the pulse oximeter",
    what: "Injury from heat, irritant gases, carbon monoxide and cyanide after fire exposure, such as a burning vehicle or building.",
    why: "Heat and irritants damage the upper and lower airway, with swelling that can worsen over hours. Carbon monoxide binds hemoglobin and gives a falsely normal SpO2. Cyanide from burning plastics blocks cellular oxygen use.",
    signs: ["Soot around the nose and mouth, singed whiskers", "Coughing, noisy breathing, rapid breathing", "Bright red or pale gums", "Confusion, weakness, seizures", "Burned paw pads or eyes"],
    care: ["Removal from smoke and high-flow oxygen, with the dog allowed to breathe without a muzzle", "Early intubation considered when airway swelling is developing", "Hydroxocobalamin for suspected cyanide toxicity is a veterinary-directed option", "Eye flushing and burn care", "Monitoring for delayed lung injury over 24 to 72 hours"],
    warn: ["A normal SpO2 does not exclude carbon monoxide poisoning", "Airway swelling can close the airway after a dog first seems stable", "Hydroxocobalamin turns urine and plasma red and interferes with some lab tests"] },

  { id: "k9-eye", name: "Eye injury", line: "Dogs rub, scratch and make it worse",
    what: "Corneal scratches, foreign bodies, chemical splashes, blunt injury and penetrating eye wounds. Dogs working in brush, sand and rubble are at high risk.",
    why: "Dogs paw at painful eyes and rub them on the ground. Penetrating injuries risk loss of the eye and infection, and pressure on the globe can push eye contents out.",
    signs: ["Squinting, tearing, pawing at the eye", "Cloudy or bluish cornea", "Visible foreign body or a misshapen globe", "Eye bulging out of the socket (proptosis), especially after head trauma"],
    care: ["Muzzle and head control before examination", "Flushing with clean water or saline for debris or chemicals", "Antibiotics for penetrating eye injuries per the K9TCCC Guidelines", "An Elizabethan cone (e-collar) or protective goggles prevent rubbing", "Urgent veterinary evaluation for penetrating injuries and proptosis"],
    warn: ["The K9TCCC Guidelines advise against bandaging or covering a penetrating eye injury", "Ketamine can raise eye pressure; alternatives are preferred in penetrating eye trauma", "Pressure on the globe is avoided"] },

  { id: "k9-paw", name: "Paw and pad injuries", line: "Small wounds that bleed and end a mission",
    what: "Cuts, burns, punctures and abrasions of the footpads and webbing from glass, metal, rock, hot pavement and chemical contact. Torn nails and dewclaws are also common.",
    why: "Pads are thick and well supplied with blood, so cuts bleed heavily and heal slowly. Every step reopens the wound, and contamination is constant.",
    signs: ["Limping or holding a paw up", "Licking or chewing at the foot", "Bleeding, bloody paw prints", "Blistered or peeling pads after hot or chemical surfaces"],
    care: ["Bleeding controlled with direct pressure and a padded pressure bandage", "Wounds flushed with clean water or saline", "Padded bandage extending from the toes to above the wrist or hock to avoid a tourniquet effect", "Bandage checked often for swelling of the toes, odor or wetness", "Booties or rest as directed by veterinary staff"],
    warn: ["A bandage that slips or is too tight cuts off circulation to the toes", "Wet bandages lead to skin damage and infection", "Dogs chew bandages and swallow the material"] },

  { id: "k9-fracture", name: "Fractures", line: "Splints work below the elbow and knee",
    what: "Broken bones from blast, falls, vehicles and gunshots. Open fractures are common in combat injuries.",
    why: "Fractures below the elbow and knee can be splinted. Upper limb bones (humerus and femur) are surrounded by heavy muscle and are hard to splint; a poorly made splint ending mid-limb acts as a lever and worsens injury.",
    signs: ["Limb not bearing weight, abnormal angle or movement", "Swelling, crepitus, severe pain on handling", "Bone visible in a wound"],
    care: ["Hemorrhage control and covering open fractures with a sterile dressing", "Antibiotics for open fractures per the K9TCCC Guidelines", "Padded splint including the joint above and below for fractures below the elbow or knee", "Confinement to a crate or litter for humerus and femur fractures", "Pain scored and treated under the guidelines"],
    warn: ["A splint ending at the fracture can make it worse", "Toes left visible to check circulation", "Spine injuries are handled on a flat board"] },

  { id: "k9-hypogly", name: "Hypoglycemia", line: "Weakness and seizures from low blood sugar",
    what: "Low blood glucose from xylitol, prolonged hard work without food, heat stroke, sepsis and some toxins.",
    why: "The brain relies on glucose. Low blood sugar causes weakness, trembling and seizures, and prolonged hypoglycemia can cause lasting brain injury.",
    signs: ["Weakness, wobbliness, trembling", "Dull or confused behavior, apparent blindness", "Seizures, collapse"],
    care: ["Blood glucose measured with a glucometer; veterinary glucometers are calibrated for dogs, and human meters can read differently", "IV dextrose under veterinary direction", "Sugar or syrup rubbed on the gums is described in veterinary first aid for a dog that cannot have an IV, with care to protect the airway and the provider's fingers", "Search for the cause, such as xylitol or heat stroke"],
    warn: ["Liquids are not poured into the mouth of a dog that cannot swallow", "Repeat checks needed because blood sugar can fall again", "Seizing dogs bite reflexively"] },

  { id: "k9-gi", name: "Vomiting, diarrhea and dehydration", line: "Common illness that drains a working dog",
    what: "Gastrointestinal illness from dietary change, scavenging, contaminated water, parasites, infections such as parvovirus and toxins. Dehydration follows from losses and poor intake, worsened by heat.",
    why: "Fluid loss reduces performance and heat tolerance and can progress to shock. Repeated vomiting also raises aspiration risk and can hide a more serious problem such as foreign body obstruction or GDV.",
    signs: ["Vomiting or diarrhea, sometimes with blood", "Dry or tacky gums, slow capillary refill", "Skin tent that returns slowly (less reliable in older dogs)", "Sunken eyes, lethargy, weight loss"],
    care: ["Rest from work and shade", "Small amounts of water by mouth for mild cases as directed by veterinary staff", "IV or subcutaneous fluids under veterinary direction for moderate to severe dehydration", "Antiemetics such as maropitant are veterinary-directed", "Stool and vomit described on the record for the veterinarian"],
    warn: ["Unproductive retching suggests GDV rather than simple vomiting", "Human antidiarrheal products such as bismuth subsalicylate contain salicylate", "Bloody diarrhea with collapse is an emergency"] },

  { id: "k9-decon", name: "Explosive, chemical and hazardous exposure", line: "Dogs lick what lands on their coat",
    what: "Exposure to explosive residues, fuels, toxic industrial chemicals, chemical warfare agents and drug powders during detection work.",
    why: "A dog's coat holds contaminants, and grooming turns skin exposure into ingestion. Paws contact contaminated ground with every step. Nerve agents and organophosphate pesticides cause drooling, tremors, small pupils, vomiting and seizures, as in people.",
    signs: ["Drooling, vomiting, tremors, small pupils with nerve agents or pesticides", "Red, irritated skin, eyes or mouth", "Coughing or breathing difficulty", "Odor of fuel or chemical on the coat"],
    care: ["Decontamination with copious water and mild dish soap, rinsing to the skin", "Eye flushing and mouth rinsing without forcing water into the airway", "Handlers and medics in protective equipment during decontamination", "Atropine and pralidoxime for nerve agent exposure are veterinary-directed treatments", "Contaminated collars and gear removed and bagged"],
    warn: ["Bleach and solvents are not applied to skin", "A wet dog after decontamination chills quickly", "Vomiting is not induced after caustic or fuel ingestion"] }
];

var FM_K9_DRUGS = [
  ["Naloxone", "Opioid antagonist used in dogs for opioid overdose, accidental fentanyl exposure in detection dogs and reversal of opioids given for pain. K9 naloxone kits (intranasal or injectable) are commonly carried by handlers, and the K9TCCC Guidelines call for naloxone to be available whenever opioids are used.", "Can wear off before a long-acting or potent opioid, so re-sedation occurs; does not reverse xylazine. Handlers exposed to the same powder may also need it."],
  ["Ketamine", "Listed in the K9TCCC Guidelines for pain and sedation, usually combined with an opioid or midazolam in dogs to limit dissociative effects. Used widely in veterinary anesthesia, as in human medicine.", "Can cause nystagmus, muscle rigidity and raised eye pressure; alternatives are preferred in penetrating eye trauma per the guidelines."],
  ["Midazolam", "Listed in the K9TCCC Guidelines as part of combination sedation, and in the JTS MWD CPG for seizures (with diazepam). Same drug class and uses as in people.", "Given alone to a healthy, alert dog it can cause paradoxical excitement; benzodiazepines alone are not recommended for analgesia."],
  ["Fentanyl, hydromorphone and morphine", "Injectable opioids listed in the K9TCCC Guidelines for moderate to severe pain. Dogs need considerably higher per-weight doses than people.", "Vomiting is common (more with morphine and hydromorphone than fentanyl), and hydromorphone causes heavy panting; oral transmucosal fentanyl lozenges are not given to dogs. Slow heart rate and drowsiness are expected effects."],
  ["Acetaminophen", "Not in the K9TCCC Guidelines. Veterinarians occasionally use it in dogs under specific dosing; it is extremely toxic to cats.", "Narrower margin in dogs than in people; overdose causes liver injury, methemoglobinemia (brown gums), facial swelling and dry eye. Human products may contain other drugs or xylitol."],
  ["Ibuprofen, naproxen and aspirin (human NSAIDs)", "Not used for dogs in the K9TCCC Guidelines. Human over-the-counter NSAIDs are a leading cause of canine poisoning calls.", "Dogs develop stomach ulcers and kidney injury at relatively low exposures; naproxen has a much longer half-life in dogs than in humans."],
  ["Meloxicam and other veterinary NSAIDs", "Veterinary NSAIDs such as meloxicam and carprofen are approved for dogs for pain and inflammation. Meloxicam was removed from the K9TCCC Guidelines for mild to moderate pain.", "Risk of GI ulcers and kidney injury is higher in a dehydrated, hypotensive or bleeding dog; not combined with steroids or another NSAID."],
  ["Tranexamic acid (TXA)", "In the K9TCCC Guidelines for dogs expected to need significant transfusion or with significant head injury after blast or blunt trauma, given by slow IV push within 3 hours of injury. Canine trauma evidence is limited and largely extrapolated from human trials.", "Rapid IV injection in dogs can cause vomiting and low blood pressure; TXA is a recognized emetic in dogs in veterinary studies."],
  ["Canine blood products", "Canine whole blood, red cells and plasma are first-line resuscitation fluids in the K9TCCC Guidelines, held by veterinary facilities. Dogs are typed for DEA 1.", "Human blood products and human albumin are not given to dogs; a dog transfused more than 72 hours before injury needs veterinary direction because of sensitization."],
  ["Crystalloids and hetastarch", "Balanced crystalloids (lactated Ringer's, Normosol-R, Plasma-Lyte A) are the last step of the K9TCCC shock sequence and the main fluid for heat stroke. Hetastarch appears in the older JTS MWD CPG but was removed from K9TCCC burn resuscitation.", "Large volumes dilute clotting factors; potassium-containing fluids are not given as a rapid bolus when potassium is high. Starches carry kidney and clotting concerns."],
  ["Calcium", "Listed in the K9TCCC Guidelines after the second transfused blood product, because citrate in stored blood binds calcium, as in human resuscitation.", "Given slowly with heart rate monitoring; rapid injection causes slow heart rate and arrhythmias, and it is not mixed in the same line as blood."],
  ["Hypertonic saline", "Listed in the K9TCCC Guidelines for dogs with head injury and signs of impending brain herniation. Also used by veterinarians for shock in some settings.", "Raises sodium; tissue injury occurs if it leaks outside the vein, and fluid overload is a risk in dogs with heart or lung problems."],
  ["Dextrose", "Used by veterinarians for hypoglycemia from xylitol, heat stroke, sepsis or exhaustion, as in people.", "Concentrated dextrose irritates veins and is typically diluted; blood glucose can fall again after a single correction, especially with xylitol."],
  ["Epinephrine", "Used in dogs for anaphylaxis and cardiac arrest, listed in the JTS MWD CPG for CPR.", "Canine anaphylaxis centers on the liver and gut, with vomiting, diarrhea and collapse more typical than throat swelling; arrhythmias can follow."],
  ["Diphenhydramine", "A human antihistamine commonly used by veterinarians in dogs for mild allergic reactions and stings. Not listed in the K9TCCC Guidelines.", "Causes sedation or occasionally excitement. Human combination products may contain pseudoephedrine, phenylephrine, acetaminophen or xylitol, all hazardous to dogs. Does not replace epinephrine in anaphylaxis."],
  ["Maropitant", "A veterinary NK-1 receptor antagonist (Cerenia) approved for vomiting and motion sickness in dogs. Veterinarians often give it before opioids to reduce vomiting.", "No human equivalent in most aid bags; the injection stings under the skin. Stopping vomiting can mask an obstruction or toxin."],
  ["Ondansetron", "A human antiemetic used off-label in dogs by veterinarians for nausea and vomiting.", "Less effective for some types of canine vomiting than maropitant; like any antiemetic it can mask GDV or obstruction."],
  ["Ceftriaxone, cefotaxime, ertapenem and moxifloxacin", "The antibiotics listed in the K9TCCC Guidelines for open combat wounds and penetrating eye injuries (moxifloxacin by mouth when the dog can take pills). Similar to the human TCCC approach.", "Dogs are dosed by weight on veterinary schedules; fluoroquinolones are avoided in growing puppies because of cartilage damage."],
  ["Cefazolin and ampicillin", "Common veterinary antibiotics for surgical prophylaxis and wound infection in dogs, used in veterinary facilities rather than the K9TCCC prehospital list.", "Allergic reactions occur in dogs as in people; ampicillin by mouth is poorly absorbed compared with injection."],
  ["Dexamethasone", "Used by veterinarians in dogs for some allergic, inflammatory and spinal conditions. The JTS MWD CPG lists steroids among cerebral edema drugs, but they are not indicated for lung contusions.", "Raises risk of stomach ulcers, especially combined with NSAIDs; impairs healing and raises blood sugar. Not a TBI treatment in current human evidence."],
  ["Apomorphine and hydrogen peroxide", "Apomorphine is the veterinary drug of choice to induce vomiting in dogs; 3 percent hydrogen peroxide by mouth is an older home method. Both are used only under veterinary or poison control direction.", "Inducing vomiting is unsafe after caustics, fuels or sharp objects, or in a drowsy, seizing or already vomiting dog. Hydrogen peroxide can cause stomach ulceration and aspiration."],
  ["Activated charcoal", "Used by veterinarians to bind some swallowed toxins, such as certain NSAIDs and methylxanthines, after veterinary or poison control consultation.", "Aspiration risk in a drowsy dog; some products with sorbitol and repeated doses can cause dangerous high sodium in dogs. Binds xylitol, alcohols, metals and caustics poorly."],
  ["Xylazine and dexmedetomidine", "Alpha-2 agonist sedatives used in veterinary practice; not in the K9TCCC Guidelines. Xylazine also appears as an adulterant in illicit opioids.", "Cause marked slow heart rate, pale gums from vasoconstriction (confusing shock assessment) and vomiting; avoided in shocked or unstable dogs. Naloxone does not reverse them."],
  ["Atipamezole", "A veterinary alpha-2 antagonist that reverses dexmedetomidine and xylazine in dogs; used for reversal of veterinary sedation and has been reported for xylazine exposure.", "Rapid reversal can cause sudden waking, excitement or aggression in a dog not yet controlled; pain relief from the sedative is lost."],
  ["Hydroxocobalamin", "A cyanide antidote used in humans after smoke inhalation; veterinary use in dogs for suspected cyanide toxicity is reported and directed by a veterinarian.", "Turns skin, urine and plasma deep red, which interferes with colorimetric lab tests and can confuse membrane color assessment."]
];

var FM_K9_QUIZ = [
  ["Which normal resting rectal temperature range best fits a healthy dog?", ["97.0 to 98.6 F", "98.6 to 99.5 F", "101 to 102.5 F", "104 to 105.5 F"], 2, "Dogs normally run warmer than people, about 101 to 102.5 F. Working dogs can briefly reach about 106 F during hard work."],
  ["Why is a respiratory rate count often unreliable in a working dog?", ["Dogs breathe only through the mouth", "Panting is a normal cooling behavior that raises the count", "Dogs hold their breath when handled", "Dog chest movement cannot be seen"], 1, "Panting is how dogs shed heat. Effort, noise and posture tell more about breathing difficulty than the raw count."],
  ["A dog has black (pigmented) gums. Where else can mucous membrane color and capillary refill be assessed?", ["The paw pad", "The ear tip skin", "The nose leather", "The inner lip, conjunctiva, prepuce or vulva"], 3, "Pigmented gums hide color. Unpigmented mucous membranes, such as the inner lip, conjunctiva, prepuce or vulva, are alternatives."],
  ["Where is the femoral pulse felt on a dog?", ["On the inside of the thigh", "On the outside of the hock", "Behind the ear", "Under the tail"], 0, "The femoral artery runs on the inside (medial) thigh. Its strength helps judge perfusion and shock."],
  ["In which situation is a muzzle contraindicated?", ["A calm dog with a paw cut", "A dog in respiratory distress or vomiting", "A dog with a tail wound", "A dog being moved to a litter"], 1, "The K9TCCC Guidelines describe muzzling unless there is airway obstruction or breathing difficulty. Vomiting and heat injury also make a closed muzzle dangerous."],
  ["Why do the K9TCCC Guidelines consider only elastic tourniquets for dog limbs?", ["Dogs bleed less than people", "Elastic tourniquets are cheaper", "Windlass tourniquets are too heavy", "Windlass tourniquets tend to slip down the cone-shaped dog limb"], 3, "The canine limb tapers toward the paw, so rigid windlass tourniquets slide down and lose pressure. An elastic wrap acts as a tight circumferential pressure bandage."],
  ["How are junctional wounds of the axilla or groin managed in K9TCCC?", ["Junctional tourniquet", "Pelvic binder", "Hemostatic gauze packing with firm direct pressure", "Wound left open to drain"], 2, "Junctional tourniquets and pelvic binders are not recommended for dogs. Packing with hemostatic gauze and direct pressure is the approach."],
  ["Where is the surgical airway made in a dog per the K9TCCC Guidelines?", ["Between tracheal rings on the front of the neck", "Through the cricothyroid membrane", "Through the side of the neck", "Through the mouth floor"], 0, "The guidelines describe an incision between tracheal rings, such as rings 3 and 4 or 4 and 5, rather than through the cricothyroid membrane."],
  ["Which airway devices do the K9TCCC Guidelines describe as not used in dogs?", ["Endotracheal tubes", "Tracheostomy tubes", "Bag-valve devices", "Nasopharyngeal airways and extraglottic devices"], 3, "Nasopharyngeal airways, blind-insertion airways and extraglottic devices are not used. Orotracheal intubation under direct vision is the main advanced airway."],
  ["Where is needle decompression performed on a dog in the K9TCCC Guidelines?", ["2nd intercostal space, front of the chest", "7th to 9th intercostal space on the side of the chest", "Just behind the last rib on the belly", "Under the front leg at the armpit crease"], 1, "The lateral chest at the 7th to 9th intercostal space, about midway up, angled toward the head, with the catheter fed off the needle. Both sides are often decompressed."],
  ["Why is a pneumothorax in a dog often bilateral?", ["The canine mediastinum is thin and often communicates between sides", "Dogs have one lung", "Dogs have no diaphragm", "Dog ribs are flexible"], 0, "The thin, often incomplete mediastinum lets air move between the two sides of the chest."],
  ["What is the most common IV site in a conscious dog?", ["Jugular vein", "Ear vein", "Cephalic vein on the front of the forearm", "Tail vein"], 2, "The cephalic vein is the usual site. The jugular is avoided in head injury and bleeding disorders."],
  ["Which IO site do the K9TCCC Guidelines describe when IV access fails?", ["Sternum", "Distal radius at the paw", "Skull", "Proximal medial tibia just below the knee"], 3, "The proximal medial tibia is the listed site; the proximal humerus is also described in the JTS MWD CPG."],
  ["Which blood product is NOT given to a dog?", ["Human low titer O whole blood", "Canine plasma", "Canine whole blood", "Canine red cells"], 0, "Human blood products carry a high risk of transfusion reaction in dogs. Only canine products are used."],
  ["Why might a dog tolerate its first mismatched transfusion but react to a later one?", ["Dogs make antibodies only to human blood", "Dogs generally lack strong naturally occurring antibodies but become sensitized after transfusion", "Dog red cells have no antigens", "Dogs only have one blood type"], 1, "Dogs usually lack strong preformed antibodies against DEA 1. After exposure they become sensitized, which is why prior transfusion more than 72 hours earlier calls for veterinary direction."],
  ["Per the JTS K9 heat injury CPG, at about what rectal temperature does active cooling stop?", ["99 F", "101 F", "103 to 103.5 F", "105 F"], 2, "Active cooling stops at about 103 to 103.5 F to avoid rebound hypothermia, followed by drying and close monitoring."],
  ["Which cooling method does the JTS K9 heat injury CPG describe?", ["Ice-water immersion", "Oxygen mask and rest", "Covering with wet towels left in place", "Soaking the coat to the skin with tepid water and using fans"], 3, "Tepid water soaked through the coat plus airflow is the described method. Wet towels left in place and masks trap heat."],
  ["A German Shepherd is retching without producing anything and has a tight, swollen belly. What is the leading concern?", ["Gastric dilatation-volvulus", "Xylitol poisoning", "Heat cramps", "Kennel cough"], 0, "Unproductive retching with a distended abdomen in a large, deep-chested dog is classic for GDV, a surgical emergency."],
  ["Why does xylitol cause hypoglycemia in dogs?", ["It blocks glucose absorption", "It damages the pancreas immediately", "It triggers a large, rapid insulin release in dogs", "It is converted to alcohol"], 2, "In dogs, unlike people, xylitol stimulates a strong insulin surge, dropping blood sugar within about 10 to 60 minutes. Liver failure can follow."],
  ["Which human pain reliever is a common cause of stomach ulcers and kidney injury in dogs?", ["Lidocaine", "Naproxen", "Ondansetron", "Diphenhydramine"], 1, "Human NSAIDs such as ibuprofen and naproxen cause GI ulceration and kidney injury in dogs; naproxen lasts much longer in dogs than in people."],
  ["A detection dog becomes suddenly sedated with slow breathing after a search. Naloxone produces little response. What else is considered?", ["Xylitol", "Hypothermia only", "Chocolate", "Xylazine or another non-opioid sedative"], 3, "Illicit drug supplies may contain xylazine, which naloxone does not reverse. Re-sedation after naloxone can also occur as it wears off."],
  ["Which side effect is commonly seen when dogs receive opioids such as hydromorphone?", ["Vomiting and panting", "Diarrhea and hair loss", "Fever and rash", "Rapid heart rate and hypertension"], 0, "Opioids commonly cause vomiting and hydromorphone causes heavy panting in dogs, which is why a muzzled dog is watched closely after opioid dosing."],
  ["Why do the K9TCCC Guidelines prefer alternatives to ketamine in penetrating eye trauma?", ["Ketamine causes blindness", "Ketamine can cause nystagmus and raise intraocular pressure", "Ketamine is toxic to dogs", "Ketamine is not used in dogs"], 1, "Ketamine can raise eye pressure and cause nystagmus, so alternatives are preferred with a penetrating eye injury."],
  ["Which form travels with an injured MWD through each handoff?", ["DD Form 1380 only", "SF 600", "K9TCCC Card, DD Form 3073", "DA Form 7656"], 2, "The K9TCCC Card (DD Form 3073) stays with the dog and later goes to the veterinary record and the DoD MWD Trauma Registry."],
  ["Under the JTS K9 Healthcare Provider Responsibilities CPG, when do human providers give emergency care to an MWD?", ["When the dog is too unstable to move, evacuation is not possible and veterinary staff are unavailable", "For all routine and dental care", "Only after a veterinarian arrives on scene", "Only at Role 4 facilities"], 0, "Human providers address threats to life, limb or eyesight and prepare the dog for evacuation, under veterinary guidance, when no veterinary personnel are available."]
];

