var FM_RAD_TOPICS = [
  ["Contamination is not the same as dose", "Contamination means radioactive material is on or in a person. Dose means energy was deposited in their tissue. Someone can be heavily contaminated with very little dose, or have received a lethal dose with no contamination at all.\nA casualty who received external radiation and is not contaminated is not radioactive and poses no hazard to anyone. This single point is responsible for more delayed care in radiation events than any other misunderstanding.\nContamination is found with a survey meter. Dose is estimated from the event, the time to vomiting, serial lymphocyte counts and specialist reconstruction."],
  ["Time, distance and shielding", "Dose falls with the time spent near a source, rises and falls steeply with distance, and is reduced by whatever mass is between the person and the source.\nDoubling the distance from a point source cuts the dose to roughly a quarter. Moving away is usually the fastest and most effective protective action available.\nAfter a detonation, a substantial building blocks a large fraction of the fallout dose, which is why sheltering for the first hours beats moving through the fallout."],
  ["Time to vomiting as a field triage tool", "In a mass-casualty radiation event there is no dosimeter on most casualties. The interval from exposure to the first vomit is the practical substitute.\nVomiting within an hour suggests a high dose. Vomiting after six hours, or not at all, suggests a low one. It is crude, it is confounded by fear and by other injuries, and it is still the best thing available forward.\nIt can only be recorded at the time. Writing it down on every casualty, with a clock time, is a deliberate step."],
  ["Serial lymphocyte counts", "Lymphocytes are exquisitely radiosensitive and fall predictably with dose. A baseline count and counts every 6 to 12 hours through the first 48 hours give a dose estimate.\nThe first count is often normal, which is why one count is not useful and serial counts are. The rate of fall carries the information.\nThis is the Andrews lymphocyte depletion method, and it sorts casualties into treatment groups when nothing else can."],
  ["Life-saving care comes before decontamination", "Radiation injury kills over days and weeks. Hemorrhage, airway obstruction and tension pneumothorax kill over minutes.\nStandard trauma care is given first. Removing the casualty's clothing takes away most external contamination and takes seconds, and the rest of decontamination waits until the casualty is stable.\nTeam protection during that period is standard precautions with respiratory protection, which is adequate for essentially every contaminated-casualty scenario."],
  ["The countermeasures are isotope-specific", "There is no general radiation antidote, and each available drug addresses one narrow problem.\nPotassium iodide blocks thyroid uptake of radioiodine only. Prussian blue binds cesium and thallium in the gut. DTPA chelates plutonium, americium, curium and some other transuranics. Colony-stimulating factors support the marrow after a significant whole-body dose.\nNone of them treats external dose, and giving the wrong one wastes the window for the right one. Identifying the isotope is therefore a medical question, not just a scene question."],
  ["Combined injury", "A casualty with trauma or burns plus a significant radiation dose does substantially worse than either injury predicts alone.\nWounds in an irradiated casualty become infected and heal poorly, and the marrow that would normally support recovery is failing. Burns and radiation together are particularly lethal.\nThe practical consequence is that surgery, if it is going to happen, happens early, before the counts fall. The window is roughly the first 48 hours."],
  ["Responder protection and dosimetry", "Responders working a radiological scene wear dosimeters and work to turn-back doses set by command. Standard precautions plus respiratory protection handle contamination; nothing in normal protective equipment stops penetrating gamma.\nThe protection that works is time, distance and shielding, applied to the responder as much as to the casualty.\nEating, drinking and smoking in a contaminated area are the routes by which responders take material internally."]
];

var FM_RAD_QUIZ = [
  ["A casualty received a significant whole-body dose from an external source and is not contaminated. What hazard do they pose to the team?", ["None. External irradiation does not make a person radioactive", "They emit gamma radiation and need lead shielding", "They are a contact hazard until washed", "Their exhaled air is contaminated"], 0, "Irradiation and contamination are different things. A casualty exposed to external penetrating radiation is not a source, and delaying their care on that belief is the most common error in radiation events."],
  ["In a mass-casualty radiation event with no dosimetry, what single piece of information most helps triage?", ["The interval from exposure to the first episode of vomiting", "The casualty's blood pressure on arrival", "Whether their hair has started to fall out", "The reading on a survey meter held to their chest"], 0, "Time to vomiting correlates with dose: within an hour suggests a high dose, after six hours or not at all a low one. It can only be captured at the time, so it is recorded on every casualty."],
  ["Why are serial lymphocyte counts used rather than a single count?", ["The first count is often normal, and the rate of fall carries the dose information", "Lymphocytes rise with dose, so a trend is needed to see it", "A single count is unreliable because of laboratory error", "Lymphocytes are only affected above 10 Gy"], 0, "Lymphocytes fall predictably with dose over the first 48 hours, but the initial count is frequently normal. The Andrews method uses counts every 6 to 12 hours to estimate dose from the slope."],
  ["What does potassium iodide protect against?", ["Thyroid uptake of radioactive iodine only", "All internal radioactive contamination", "External gamma dose", "Cesium and strontium uptake"], 0, "It saturates the thyroid so radioiodine is not taken up. It does nothing for other isotopes or for external dose, and the belief that it is a general radiation antidote leads people to take it instead of evacuating."],
  ["A contaminated casualty arrives with uncontrolled external hemorrhage. What comes first?", ["Hemorrhage control, with the team in standard precautions", "Full decontamination before any treatment", "A survey meter reading of the whole body", "Potassium iodide"], 0, "Contamination has not killed anyone in the first hour and hemorrhage does. Standard precautions with respiratory protection protect the team adequately while life-saving care is given; clothing removal then takes away most of the contamination."],
  ["Which countermeasure matches cesium-137 internal contamination?", ["Prussian blue", "Ca-DTPA", "Potassium iodide", "Dimercaprol"], 0, "Prussian blue binds cesium and thallium in the gut and interrupts their reabsorption, shortening the time the body is irradiated. The pharmaceutical-grade product is used; artists' pigment is not a substitute."],
  ["Which countermeasure matches inhaled plutonium or americium?", ["Pentetate calcium trisodium (Ca-DTPA), as early as possible", "Prussian blue", "Potassium iodide", "Sodium bicarbonate"], 0, "DTPA chelates transuranics. Ca-DTPA is first-line in the first 24 hours and works best in the first hours, so it is started before confirmatory bioassay rather than after it."],
  ["Why are nasal swabs taken immediately on a contaminated casualty?", ["They estimate inhalation, and become useless once the face is washed", "They are the only way to detect gamma emitters", "They replace urine bioassay", "They determine whether potassium iodide is needed"], 0, "Nasal swabs give an early estimate of how much material was inhaled. Taken after facial decontamination they tell you nothing, so they are done on arrival."],
  ["What makes a criticality accident different from other radiation exposures?", ["Neutron activation makes some of the casualty's own body and metal items briefly radioactive", "It produces only alpha radiation", "The dose is always survivable", "Contamination is the main hazard"], 0, "The neutron component activates sodium in the casualty's blood and metal items they carry. Blood sodium-24 and activated rings, buckles and coins give a physical dose estimate, so those items are collected rather than discarded."],
  ["After a nuclear detonation, what is the best protective action for people downwind in the first hours?", ["Shelter inside a substantial building", "Evacuate immediately on foot", "Take potassium iodide and continue working", "Drive upwind regardless of the route"], 0, "Moving through fallout in the first hours usually delivers more dose than staying inside a substantial structure. Sheltering for roughly the first 12 to 24 hours, then evacuating on direction, blocks far more dose."],
  ["A casualty has marrow failure and hair loss, no known radiation exposure, and a normal gamma survey meter reading. What should be considered?", ["An alpha emitter such as polonium-210, which a gamma meter does not detect", "Thermal burn injury", "A nerve agent exposure", "Nothing further; the clean survey rules out radiation"], 0, "Pure alpha emitters give no reading on a standard gamma survey meter while irradiating the body from within. A clean survey is not clearance, and alpha spectrometry of urine has to be requested specifically."],
  ["Why is early surgery favored in a casualty with combined radiation and traumatic injury?", ["The window closes as blood counts fall over the following days", "Radiation makes anesthesia safer early", "Wounds heal faster in the first 48 hours after irradiation", "Surgery reduces the radiation dose"], 0, "An irradiated casualty's marrow is failing, so infection risk rises and healing worsens as the days pass. If surgery is going to happen it happens in roughly the first 48 hours, before the counts drop."],
  ["What is the characteristic timing of a cutaneous radiation injury?", ["An early flush that fades, then the real injury at two to three weeks", "Immediate blistering like a thermal burn", "Injury only at six months", "Pain at the time of exposure with no later changes"], 0, "The early erythema settles and gives a false reassurance, then redness, blistering and breakdown appear at two to three weeks and evolve in waves for months. Treating it as a thermal burn underestimates the depth."],
  ["Which statement about a radiological dispersal device is accurate?", ["The blast causes the deaths; radiation causes contamination, fear and a long cleanup", "Most casualties develop acute radiation syndrome", "Decontamination precedes trauma care", "Potassium iodide is given to everyone at the scene"], 0, "The casualties are trauma casualties. Acute radiation syndrome is uncommon after a dispersal device, and the largest medical burden is screening and informing a very large number of uninjured people."],
  ["Which isotope behaves like calcium and lodges in bone?", ["Strontium-90", "Cesium-137", "Iodine-131", "Americium-241"], 0, "Strontium follows the calcium pathway into bone, where it irradiates the adjacent marrow for decades. There is no effective chelator, so the useful actions are reducing gut absorption early and calcium loading under specialist direction."]
];

/* Known treatment for each biological and chemical agent, matched against what the Combat Paramedic
   tier actually carries. Written by the owner, Oct 29 2026. Each row is [drug id, what it does for this agent].
   Rows whose drug is outside this tier's carried list render as "treatment you would request or receive". */
var FM_AGENT_CARRY = {
  /* ---------- Biological ---------- */
  anthrax: [["ciprofloxacin", "A fluoroquinolone is a first-line backbone for treatment and for 60-day prophylaxis after exposure."], ["doxycycline", "The alternate backbone, and the usual oral prophylaxis when a fluoroquinolone does not fit."], ["moxifloxacin", "Carried at this tier. A fluoroquinolone already in the bag covers the backbone until the named regimen arrives."], ["meropenem", "Added as a second bactericidal drug in systemic disease."], ["clindamycin", "Added as a protein-synthesis inhibitor to cut toxin production."], ["lactated-ringers", "Shock from hemorrhagic mediastinitis is fluid- and pressor-dependent."]],
  plague: [["gentamicin", "An aminoglycoside is a first-line choice for pneumonic and septicemic plague."], ["streptomycin", "The classic first-line aminoglycoside where it is stocked."], ["ciprofloxacin", "A fluoroquinolone is first-line and is the usual oral post-exposure prophylaxis for 7 days."], ["doxycycline", "Alternate treatment and alternate prophylaxis."], ["moxifloxacin", "Carried at this tier, in the same fluoroquinolone class."], ["norepinephrine", "Septicemic plague is a shock problem as much as an antibiotic problem."]],
  tularemia: [["gentamicin", "An aminoglycoside is first-line for severe disease."], ["streptomycin", "The traditional first-line agent."], ["ciprofloxacin", "Used for mild disease and for post-exposure prophylaxis."], ["doxycycline", "Alternate oral option, with a longer course and more relapse."]],
  brucella: [["doxycycline", "The backbone of every accepted regimen, given for six weeks."], ["rifampin", "Paired with doxycycline in the common oral regimen."], ["gentamicin", "Added for the first one to two weeks in severe or focal disease."]],
  qfever: [["doxycycline", "The treatment of choice, and what post-exposure prophylaxis uses if started in the right window."]],
  glanders: [["meropenem", "A carbapenem is the intensive-phase choice."], ["ertapenem", "Carried at this tier, in the carbapenem class, until the named agent is available."], ["trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole", "The eradication-phase drug, given for months."], ["ceftriaxone", "Carried at this tier, but it does not reliably cover these organisms. It is not a substitute."]],
  smallpox: [["tecovirimat", "The antiviral held in the national stockpile for orthopoxvirus disease."], ["lactated-ringers", "Fluid losses through denuded skin drive much of the mortality."], ["cefazolin", "Carried at this tier, for the bacterial superinfection of broken skin."], ["morphine", "Carried at this tier. Pain control is a real part of this care."]],
  mpox: [["tecovirimat", "The same antiviral, used under protocol for severe disease or high-risk patients."], ["morphine", "Carried at this tier, for lesion and lymph node pain."], ["ondansetron", "Carried at this tier, for the nausea that limits oral intake."]],
  vhf: [["lactated-ringers", "Aggressive, measured fluid replacement is the single highest-yield treatment."], ["plasma-lyte", "A balanced crystalloid, which matters when losses run for days."], ["ondansetron", "Vomiting drives the volume deficit; stopping it is treatment, not comfort."], ["ribavirin", "Of use in some arenaviral and bunyaviral disease, not in filoviral disease."], ["potassium-chloride", "Carried at this tier. Potassium losses are large and replacement is part of the resuscitation."]],
  vee: [["lactated-ringers", "Supportive care only. There is no specific antiviral."], ["acetaminophen", "Carried at this tier, for fever and headache."], ["levetiracetam", "Carried at this tier, if encephalitis brings seizures."]],
  botulism: [["botulism-antitoxin-heptavalent", "The only specific treatment. It stops further paralysis but does not reverse what is done, so early release matters."], ["rocuronium", "Carried at this tier. Airway management is the care that saves the patient; paralysis for intubation is still used normally."], ["etomidate", "Carried at this tier, for induction when the airway is taken."]],
  ricin: [["lactated-ringers", "There is no antidote. Ingested ricin is a massive gastrointestinal fluid loss."], ["ondansetron", "Carried at this tier, for the vomiting that drives those losses."], ["albuterol", "Carried at this tier, for the bronchospasm of inhaled exposure."], ["methylprednisolone", "Carried at this tier, used for airway inflammation under medical direction."]],
  seb: [["lactated-ringers", "Supportive. The illness is self-limited in most casualties but the fluid deficit is real."], ["albuterol", "Carried at this tier, for wheeze."], ["acetaminophen", "Carried at this tier, for the fever that can run to 41 C (105.8 F)."]],
  t2: [["lactated-ringers", "Supportive, with burn-style attention to losses through injured skin."], ["morphine", "Carried at this tier. The skin injury is painful."], ["ondansetron", "Carried at this tier."]],
  epsilon: [["lactated-ringers", "Supportive. There is no fielded human antitoxin."], ["albuterol", "Carried at this tier, for bronchospasm."], ["norepinephrine", "Carried at this tier, if the pulmonary edema and shock progress."]],
  psittacosis: [["doxycycline", "The treatment of choice."], ["azithromycin", "The alternative when a tetracycline does not fit."], ["moxifloxacin", "Carried at this tier, with activity against this organism."]],
  typhus: [["doxycycline", "The treatment of choice, and it works fast enough that improvement helps confirm the diagnosis."], ["azithromycin", "The alternative in pregnancy."]],
  cholera: [["lactated-ringers", "Rehydration is the treatment. Antibiotics only shorten it."], ["plasma-lyte", "A balanced solution for the very large volumes involved."], ["doxycycline", "A single dose shortens shedding and illness."], ["azithromycin", "The alternative where resistance or pregnancy rules out doxycycline."], ["potassium-chloride", "Carried at this tier. Potassium loss in cholera stool is severe."]],
  foodborne: [["lactated-ringers", "Rehydration first."], ["ondansetron", "Carried at this tier."], ["ciprofloxacin", "Used selectively, since antibiotics worsen some toxin-mediated illness."]],
  crypto: [["nitazoxanide", "The only drug with an approved indication, and it works poorly without immune function."], ["lactated-ringers", "Rehydration carries the patient."]],
  nipah: [["lactated-ringers", "Supportive. There is no approved antiviral."], ["levetiracetam", "Carried at this tier, for the seizures of encephalitis."], ["ribavirin", "Used in outbreaks without established benefit."]],
  hanta: [["lactated-ringers", "Given carefully. In the pulmonary syndrome, over-resuscitation worsens the lungs."], ["norepinephrine", "Carried at this tier, and preferred over more fluid once the lungs are wet."], ["ribavirin", "Of use in the hemorrhagic-fever-with-renal-syndrome form, not the pulmonary one."]],
  abrin: [["lactated-ringers", "No antidote. The same fluid problem as ricin."], ["ondansetron", "Carried at this tier."]],
  saxitoxin: [["rocuronium", "Carried at this tier. The threat is respiratory paralysis, so the treatment is the airway and the ventilator."], ["etomidate", "Carried at this tier, for induction."], ["lactated-ringers", "Supportive while the toxin clears over hours."]],

  /* ---------- Chemical ---------- */
  hd: [["lactated-ringers", "Fluids titrated to perfusion, deliberately less than a thermal burn formula predicts."], ["morphine", "Carried at this tier. Mustard pain arrives late and runs for weeks."], ["ketamine", "Carried at this tier, for dressing changes and debridement."], ["cefazolin", "Carried at this tier, for infected blistered skin."], ["ondansetron", "Carried at this tier, for the vomiting of significant exposure."], ["albuterol", "Carried at this tier, for airway irritation and wheeze."]],
  hn: [["lactated-ringers", "The same measured approach as sulfur mustard."], ["morphine", "Carried at this tier."], ["cefazolin", "Carried at this tier, for secondary infection."], ["ondansetron", "Carried at this tier. Nitrogen mustards cause more early vomiting than sulfur mustard."]],
  lewisite: [["dimercaprol", "The specific chelator, given for systemic arsenical poisoning and used topically where those preparations exist."], ["lactated-ringers", "Lewisite shock is real capillary leak and needs more fluid than mustard."], ["morphine", "Carried at this tier. Unlike mustard, the pain is immediate."], ["cefazolin", "Carried at this tier."]],
  cx: [["lactated-ringers", "Supportive. There is no antidote; speed of decontamination is the treatment."], ["morphine", "Carried at this tier. The pain is immediate and severe."], ["ketamine", "Carried at this tier, for wound care."]],
  nerveg: [["atropine-pralidoxime-auto-injector", "The fielded answer: atropine with pralidoxime, repeated by severity under protocol."], ["atropine", "Carried at this tier. Titrated to drying secretions and easier ventilation, not to pupil size, and the total dose may be very large."], ["pralidoxime", "The oxime that reactivates the enzyme, which is where the muscle weakness improves."], ["midazolam", "Carried at this tier, for the seizures, which cause ongoing brain injury if they run."], ["diazepam", "Carried at this tier, the alternate benzodiazepine in doctrine."], ["glycopyrrolate", "Carried at this tier. It dries secretions without crossing into the brain, so it does not replace atropine in severe exposure."], ["rocuronium", "Carried at this tier. Succinylcholine is the one to question here, since cholinesterase inhibition prolongs it badly."]],
  nervev: [["atropine-pralidoxime-auto-injector", "The same fielded answer, with more repeat doses expected."], ["atropine", "Carried at this tier, in larger cumulative amounts than for the G agents."], ["pralidoxime", "Given early and often continued as an infusion, because VX keeps leaching from skin and fat."], ["midazolam", "Carried at this tier."], ["rocuronium", "Carried at this tier, and preferred to succinylcholine for the same reason."]],
  nervea: [["atropine", "Carried at this tier. The same cholinergic management, with very high cumulative doses reported."], ["atropine-pralidoxime-auto-injector", "Still the field starting point."], ["pralidoxime", "Used, though its benefit against these agents is less established."], ["midazolam", "Carried at this tier, for seizures."]],
  cyanide: [["hydroxocobalamin", "Carried at this tier, and the preferred antidote in fire smoke because it does not induce methemoglobinemia."], ["sodium-thiosulfate", "A sulfur donor, used with hydroxocobalamin or with nitrites."], ["sodium-nitrite", "Induces methemoglobin to pull cyanide off the enzyme. Avoided in smoke inhalation, where carbon monoxide is also likely."], ["amyl-nitrite", "The inhaled stopgap in older kits when no line is in."], ["sodium-bicarbonate", "Carried at this tier, for the severe acidosis."]],
  phosgene: [["lactated-ringers", "Given with restraint. Over-resuscitation worsens the pulmonary edema."], ["albuterol", "Carried at this tier, for bronchospasm."], ["methylprednisolone", "Carried at this tier, used under medical direction; the evidence is weak."], ["norepinephrine", "Carried at this tier, and preferred to more fluid once the lungs are wet."]],
  chlorine: [["albuterol", "Carried at this tier, and the highest-yield drug for most chlorine casualties."], ["ipratropium", "Carried at this tier, added for the irritant bronchospasm."], ["racemic-epinephrine", "Carried at this tier, for upper airway swelling and stridor."], ["methylprednisolone", "Carried at this tier, for airway inflammation."]],
  ammonia: [["racemic-epinephrine", "Carried at this tier. The upper airway is the threat, and it closes fast."], ["albuterol", "Carried at this tier."], ["methylprednisolone", "Carried at this tier."], ["morphine", "Carried at this tier, for the alkali burns of skin and eyes."]],
  hf: [["calcium-gluconate", "The specific treatment: topical gel, and intravenous or regional calcium for systemic fluoride."], ["calcium-chloride", "Carried at this tier. A central line is preferred for it; it is not given subcutaneously."], ["magnesium-sulfate", "Carried at this tier. Fluoride binds magnesium as well as calcium."], ["morphine", "Carried at this tier. Pain out of proportion is a sign the fluoride is still active."]],
  h2s: [["sodium-nitrite", "Induces methemoglobin, which binds sulfide. Most benefit is early."], ["lactated-ringers", "Supportive. Oxygen and removal from the space are the real treatment."], ["norepinephrine", "Carried at this tier, for the shock after a knockdown."]],
  bz: [["physostigmine", "The specific reversal agent, used under medical direction and with cardiac monitoring."], ["lorazepam", "Carried at this tier, for agitation. Antipsychotics are avoided because they worsen the anticholinergic state."], ["midazolam", "Carried at this tier, for the same purpose."], ["lactated-ringers", "Supportive. Hyperthermia from the inability to sweat is the real danger."]],
  opioidaero: [["naloxone", "Carried at this tier. Repeat doses or an infusion are expected with potent synthetics."], ["lactated-ringers", "Supportive while ventilation is assisted."], ["etomidate", "Carried at this tier, if the airway is taken for a casualty who cannot be ventilated otherwise."]],
  rca: [["lactated-ringers", "Eye irrigation and fresh air. Almost no casualty needs a drug."], ["albuterol", "Carried at this tier, for the asthmatic who does react badly."], ["tetracaine", "Topical anesthetic for examining a painful eye, where it is stocked."]],
  wp: [["lactated-ringers", "Fluids for the thermal injury, with attention to calcium and phosphate."], ["calcium-gluconate", "For the hypocalcemia that absorbed phosphorus can cause."], ["morphine", "Carried at this tier."], ["ketamine", "Carried at this tier, for the debridement these wounds need."]],
  arsine: [["lactated-ringers", "Volume to protect the kidneys from the pigment load."], ["sodium-bicarbonate", "Carried at this tier, used to alkalinize urine under medical direction."], ["dimercaprol", "Does not treat the hemolysis. It is for arsenic poisoning, which is a different problem."]],
  op: [["atropine", "Carried at this tier. Doses are often larger and run far longer than for a nerve agent, sometimes as an infusion."], ["pralidoxime", "Given early, often as an infusion, because the pesticide redistributes for days."], ["atropine-pralidoxime-auto-injector", "A reasonable field start when the pesticide is unknown."], ["midazolam", "Carried at this tier, for seizures."], ["rocuronium", "Carried at this tier, and preferred over succinylcholine."]],

  /* ---------- Chemical, second batch ---------- */
  hydrazine: [["pyridoxine", "The specific treatment for the seizures, on the same basis as isoniazid toxicity."], ["midazolam", "Carried at this tier, given alongside pyridoxine rather than instead of it."], ["lorazepam", "Carried at this tier, the alternate benzodiazepine."], ["levetiracetam", "Carried at this tier, as an added anticonvulsant under medical direction."], ["lactated-ringers", "Carried at this tier, for the burns and the supportive care."]],
  paraquat: [["lactated-ringers", "Carried at this tier. Renal elimination is the main route out, so perfusion and urine output matter."], ["ondansetron", "Carried at this tier. The vomiting is severe and prolonged."], ["morphine", "Carried at this tier, for the mouth and esophageal injury."], ["activated-charcoal", "Early gut decontamination, where the airway is protected and medical direction supports it."]],
  phosphine: [["norepinephrine", "Carried at this tier. The shock is cardiogenic and refractory, and it is the thing that kills."], ["sodium-bicarbonate", "Carried at this tier, for the severe acidosis."], ["magnesium-sulfate", "Carried at this tier, used in reported regimens for dysrhythmia with limited evidence."], ["lactated-ringers", "Carried at this tier, used with restraint given the pulmonary edema."]],
  no2: [["albuterol", "Carried at this tier, for the bronchospasm."], ["ipratropium", "Carried at this tier, added for irritant bronchospasm."], ["methylprednisolone", "Carried at this tier. The evidence is better for the delayed bronchiolitis phase than the acute one."], ["methylene-blue", "For significant methemoglobinemia, where cyanosis does not correct with oxygen."]],
  chloropicrin: [["albuterol", "Carried at this tier, particularly for the casualty with asthma."], ["methylprednisolone", "Carried at this tier, for airway inflammation."], ["racemic-epinephrine", "Carried at this tier, for upper airway swelling."], ["tetracaine", "Topical anesthetic for examining a painful eye, where it is stocked."]],
  diphosgene: [["albuterol", "Carried at this tier."], ["methylprednisolone", "Carried at this tier, under medical direction; evidence in the acute phase is weak."], ["lactated-ringers", "Carried at this tier, given with restraint."], ["norepinephrine", "Carried at this tier, preferred to more fluid once the lungs are wet."]],
  adamsite: [["ondansetron", "Carried at this tier. Prolonged vomiting is the main disabling effect."], ["morphine", "Carried at this tier, for the severe headache and chest pain."], ["acetaminophen", "Carried at this tier, for lesser symptoms."], ["lactated-ringers", "Carried at this tier, for losses from hours of vomiting."]],
  co: [["hydroxocobalamin", "Carried at this tier. In an enclosed-space fire, cyanide travels with the carbon monoxide and this treats the part oxygen cannot."], ["lactated-ringers", "Carried at this tier, supportive."], ["ondansetron", "Carried at this tier, for the nausea."], ["acetaminophen", "Carried at this tier, for the headache once poisoning is being treated."]],
  mic: [["albuterol", "Carried at this tier."], ["methylprednisolone", "Carried at this tier, for airway inflammation."], ["racemic-epinephrine", "Carried at this tier, for upper airway swelling."], ["morphine", "Carried at this tier."], ["tetracaine", "For examining a severely injured eye, where it is stocked."]],
  thallium: [["prussian-blue", "Binds thallium in the gut and interrupts reabsorption. The specific treatment, and the window matters."], ["activated-charcoal", "Early gut decontamination where the timing and airway allow."], ["morphine", "Carried at this tier. The neuropathy is severe and often needs more than opioids alone."], ["lactated-ringers", "Carried at this tier, supportive."]],
  fluoroacetate: [["midazolam", "Carried at this tier, first-line for the seizures."], ["lorazepam", "Carried at this tier, the alternate benzodiazepine."], ["levetiracetam", "Carried at this tier, as an added anticonvulsant."], ["calcium-gluconate", "Hypocalcemia is characteristic and contributes to the dysrhythmias."], ["calcium-chloride", "Carried at this tier; a central line is preferred for it."], ["magnesium-sulfate", "Carried at this tier, for the dysrhythmias."], ["sodium-bicarbonate", "Carried at this tier, for the severe acidosis."]],
  strychnine: [["midazolam", "Carried at this tier, in large doses, both to control the spasms and to sedate a casualty who is awake through them."], ["lorazepam", "Carried at this tier, the alternate benzodiazepine."], ["rocuronium", "Carried at this tier. Paralysis with ventilation stops the contraction and the hyperthermia when benzodiazepines are not enough."], ["etomidate", "Carried at this tier, for induction. Paralysis without sedation here would leave an aware, paralyzed casualty."], ["lactated-ringers", "Carried at this tier, for rhabdomyolysis."], ["sodium-bicarbonate", "Carried at this tier, used for the acidosis and rhabdomyolysis under medical direction."]],

  /* ---------- Biological, second batch ---------- */
  aflatoxin: [["dextrose", "Carried at this tier. Hypoglycemia in acute liver failure is easy to miss and immediately dangerous."], ["lactated-ringers", "Carried at this tier, supportive."], ["ondansetron", "Carried at this tier."], ["pantoprazole", "Carried at this tier, for the bleeding risk in liver failure."]],
  tetrodotoxin: [["rocuronium", "Carried at this tier. The treatment is the airway and the ventilator; survival is complete in those who are ventilated through it."], ["etomidate", "Carried at this tier, for induction."], ["midazolam", "Carried at this tier. The casualty is conscious and aware, so sedation is treatment."], ["fentanyl", "Carried at this tier, for the same reason."], ["norepinephrine", "Carried at this tier, for the hypotension and bradycardia of severe poisoning."], ["lactated-ringers", "Carried at this tier."]],
  riftvalley: [["acetaminophen", "Carried at this tier. Salicylates are avoided given the bleeding risk."], ["lactated-ringers", "Carried at this tier, supportive, and more so in the hemorrhagic form."], ["ondansetron", "Carried at this tier."], ["levetiracetam", "Carried at this tier, for the encephalitic form."]],
  flu: [["oseltamivir", "The neuraminidase inhibitor, started within 48 hours of symptoms and on suspicion rather than on a confirmed test."], ["ceftriaxone", "Carried at this tier, for the secondary bacterial pneumonia that kills many survivors of the viral illness."], ["vancomycin", "Added where staphylococcal superinfection is suspected."], ["albuterol", "Carried at this tier."], ["methylprednisolone", "Carried at this tier, used under medical direction; routine steroids in influenza are not supported."], ["acetaminophen", "Carried at this tier."]],
  lepto: [["doxycycline", "Oral treatment for mild disease, and it covers much of the same differential."], ["penicillin-g", "Intravenous treatment for severe disease with jaundice, kidney injury or bleeding."], ["ceftriaxone", "Carried at this tier, an accepted intravenous alternative for severe disease."], ["moxifloxacin", "Carried at this tier, but it is not the drug of choice here."], ["lactated-ringers", "Carried at this tier. Kidney failure is common."], ["acetaminophen", "Carried at this tier."]],
  scrubtyphus: [["doxycycline", "The treatment of choice, with the fever typically breaking within 48 hours."], ["azithromycin", "The alternative in pregnancy and where doxycycline response is reduced."], ["acetaminophen", "Carried at this tier."], ["lactated-ringers", "Carried at this tier, supportive."]],
  typhoid: [["ceftriaxone", "Carried at this tier, and first-line given widespread fluoroquinolone resistance."], ["azithromycin", "The oral first-line alternative."], ["ciprofloxacin", "Only where local susceptibility supports it; resistance has made it unreliable empirically."], ["dexamethasone", "For shock or altered mental status in severe disease, under medical direction."], ["lactated-ringers", "Carried at this tier."], ["acetaminophen", "Carried at this tier."]],
  hepaw: [["lactated-ringers", "Carried at this tier, supportive."], ["ondansetron", "Carried at this tier."], ["acetaminophen", "Carried at this tier, dosed carefully in a casualty with liver injury."], ["pantoprazole", "Carried at this tier."]],
  cocci: [["fluconazole", "The antifungal used for severe or prolonged disease and for people at higher risk of dissemination."], ["amphotericin-b", "For severe disseminated disease, under specialist direction."], ["acetaminophen", "Carried at this tier."], ["lactated-ringers", "Carried at this tier."]],
  dengue: [["lactated-ringers", "Carried at this tier, and the whole treatment: measured crystalloid in the critical phase, titrated to perfusion and urine output."], ["plasma-lyte", "Carried at this tier, a balanced alternative."], ["acetaminophen", "Carried at this tier, and the only analgesic used here; NSAIDs and aspirin worsen bleeding."], ["ondansetron", "Carried at this tier. Persistent vomiting is also a warning sign, not just a symptom."]],

  /* ---------- Radiological and nuclear ---------- */
  "ars-hemato": [["filgrastim", "A colony-stimulating factor, FDA approved for the hematopoietic syndrome and given early for a significant marrow dose."], ["ondansetron", "Carried at this tier. Controlling the prodrome lets the casualty keep fluids down."], ["lactated-ringers", "Carried at this tier, supportive."], ["cefazolin", "Carried at this tier. Neutropenic infection is what the casualty dies of weeks later."]],
  "ars-gi": [["lactated-ringers", "Carried at this tier. The fluid losses resemble cholera in volume and are the immediate threat."], ["plasma-lyte", "Carried at this tier, a balanced solution for losses running over days."], ["ondansetron", "Carried at this tier, for a prodrome that is far harder to control than in the marrow syndrome."], ["morphine", "Carried at this tier. Mucosal injury through the whole gut is severe."], ["potassium-chloride", "Carried at this tier. The electrolyte losses are large."], ["filgrastim", "Still given, because marrow failure runs alongside the gut injury."]],
  "ars-neuro": [["morphine", "Carried at this tier. The purpose of care here is comfort, so opioids are titrated to comfort."], ["midazolam", "Carried at this tier, for seizures and for distress."], ["lorazepam", "Carried at this tier, the alternate benzodiazepine."], ["ondansetron", "Carried at this tier, given generously; the vomiting is severe."]],
  cutaneous: [["morphine", "Carried at this tier. The pain is severe, neuropathic and long-running."], ["ketamine", "Carried at this tier, for dressing changes."], ["cefazolin", "Carried at this tier, for the infection these poorly healing wounds attract."], ["acetaminophen", "Carried at this tier, for lesser pain."]],
  iodine131: [["potassium-iodide", "Saturates the thyroid so radioiodine is not taken up. Most effective shortly before or within a few hours of exposure, and distributed on public health direction."]],
  cesium137: [["prussian-blue", "Binds cesium in the gut and interrupts its reabsorption, shortening the time the body is irradiated. The pharmaceutical-grade product, not artists' pigment."]],
  transuranic: [["pentetate-calcium-trisodium", "Ca-DTPA, first-line within the first 24 hours and most effective in the first hours, so it is started before confirmatory bioassay."], ["pentetate-zinc-trisodium", "Zn-DTPA, used after the first day and for prolonged courses because it depletes fewer trace metals."]],
  polonium: [["filgrastim", "Supportive care for marrow failure, as for the hematopoietic syndrome."], ["ondansetron", "Carried at this tier, for the severe early gastrointestinal illness."], ["lactated-ringers", "Carried at this tier."], ["dimercaprol", "Chelation has been used; the evidence is limited and the decision belongs with toxicology and health physics."]],
  strontium: [["calcium-gluconate", "Calcium competes with strontium on the same pathway; loading with a diuretic has been used under specialist direction."], ["calcium-chloride", "Carried at this tier; a central line is preferred for it."]],
  rdd: [["tranexamic-acid", "Carried at this tier. These are trauma casualties first, and the blast is what kills them."], ["lactated-ringers", "Carried at this tier."], ["morphine", "Carried at this tier."], ["potassium-iodide", "Only if the dispersed material included radioiodine, which is unlikely in a dispersal device."], ["prussian-blue", "If the isotope is cesium, which is the most likely choice for a dispersal device."]],
  detonation: [["tranexamic-acid", "Carried at this tier. Hemorrhage is what the casualties die of."], ["lactated-ringers", "Carried at this tier."], ["morphine", "Carried at this tier."], ["ketamine", "Carried at this tier, for the burns and the procedures they need."], ["ondansetron", "Carried at this tier, and the time of the first vomit is recorded before it is treated."], ["filgrastim", "For survivors with a significant marrow dose, started once the dose estimate supports it."], ["potassium-iodide", "On public health direction for the radioiodine in fallout."]],
  criticality: [["filgrastim", "For those in the survivable dose range."], ["ondansetron", "Carried at this tier."], ["morphine", "Carried at this tier, and the main treatment for those closest to the event."], ["midazolam", "Carried at this tier, for seizures and distress."]]
};

